-- title:
--   Schur's lemma for irreducible representations with commuting operators
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field, $\Delta$ a group, $V$ a $k$-vector space and $\rho$ a representation of $\Delta$ on $V$ over $k$, i.e. a monoid homomorphism from $\Delta$ to $\operatorname{End}_k(V)$. Assume: (i) every $k$-submodule $W \le V$ that is $\rho$-stable in the sense that $\rho(d)(x) \in W$ for all $d \in \Delta$ and all $x \in W$ is either $\bot$ or $\top$; and (ii) the operators commute, $\rho(a)\rho(b) = \rho(b)\rho(a)$ for all $a, b \in \Delta$. Then for every $v \in V$ with $v \neq 0$ and every $w \in V$ there is a unique $k$-linear endomorphism $\varphi$ of $V$ satisfying both $\varphi \rho(d) = \rho(d) \varphi$ for all $d \in \Delta$ (composition in the endomorphism ring) and $\varphi(v) = w$. Equivalently: evaluation at any nonzero vector is a bijection from the centraliser of $\rho(\Delta)$ in $\operatorname{End}_k(V)$ onto $V$. No finiteness, finite-dimensionality or algebraic closedness hypothesis is imposed on $k$, $\Delta$ or $V$.
--
--   This is Schur's lemma in the form appropriate to an irreducible representation with abelian image: the commutant acts simply transitively on $V$ in the strong sense that evaluation at a nonzero vector is bijective. It is used to identify the centraliser of such a representation as a field generated by the image of the group algebra, with $V$ a line over it, in [`Representation.centralizer_eq_adjoin_and_isField_of_isSimple_of_forall_commute`](thm.html#Representation.centralizer_eq_adjoin_and_isField_of_isSimple_of_forall_commute).
